<?xml version="1.0" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
OmacVM.app (installed as /usr/share/drirc.d/50-omacvm-chrome.conf): Chrome,
Chromium, Brave, Vivaldi and Electron get "OmacVM-GPU" as the GL renderer
instead of "virgl (<Mac GPU>)".

Skia turns instanced drawing off when the renderer names virgl (GrGLCaps.cpp,
2021, no public reason). Without it Chrome has no path atlas: every canvas
stroke and clip path becomes a CPU mask, an upload and a draw of its own, in
every frame. The app's host GL has instancing. With this string, MotionMark
at a fixed complexity in the VM (M4 Max): Paths 29-35 -> 75 fps, Suits 40-48 ->
75 fps (75 = the VM display's rate); the pictures match Chrome's Graphite.

Only these programs (the GPU process's program: "chrome", "chromium",
"brave", "vivaldi-bin", "electron" as Electron's own build and Arch's
electronNN packages name it); every other program keeps the real string,
also an Electron app shipped under its own name. Mesa matches the name where
it sets up the screen, on Wayland (GBM) and X11 (GLX) alike. Chromium's GL
(ANGLE) draws on Chrome's own GBM device, which Mesa sets up before Chrome
loads EGL, so OmacVM's EGL vendor library cannot name it by process type.
One app with the real name: force_gl_renderer= (empty) in its environment
(Mesa then skips this file). Without this file Chrome draws as before.
-->
<driconf>
  <device driver="virtio_gpu">
    <application name="Chromium-based browsers and Electron" executable_regexp="^(chrome|chromium|brave|vivaldi-bin|electron[0-9]*)$">
      <option name="force_gl_renderer" value="OmacVM-GPU"/>
    </application>
  </device>
</driconf>
